      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/db4c6694a3bc172aea8a47f1a828eac8235286b7ce6b7e327574790a6c31c270.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$",
